#![allow(clippy::excessive_precision)]
//! Golden-parity tests for cartalith-civ's settlement-suitability
//! prerequisites (`buildRouteCorridors`/`buildLandmassQuality`/
//! `buildCoastSDF`, reference HTML lines ~5903/~5970/~7462).
//! `PHASE2_SCOPE.md` milestone 6. Generated from a Node `vm` extraction
//! run against `reference/Cartalith Gen1 v2.10.html` (harness itself is
//! transient, not checked in) that calls the reference's own
//! `buildRouteCorridors()`/`buildLandmassQuality()`/`buildCoastSDF()`
//! directly (the latter with `{euclid:true}`, matching
//! `currentSettlementSuitability()`'s real production call -- the only
//! consumer in this port's scope), not a hand-composed reimplementation.
//!
//! Cases 0/1 reuse this crate's existing fixture configs (gw/gh/seed/world/
//! w_iters=12, matching `golden_parity_carve.rs`/`golden_parity_resource_
//! potentials.rs`). Cross-checked before trusting this data: this harness's
//! own `field[0..5]` matched `golden_parity_carve.rs`'s
//! `expected_field[0..5]` exactly for both, and determinism was
//! independently confirmed by running each case twice and diffing.
//!
//! **A real harness bug found and root-caused while extracting this
//! milestone's data**: the first extraction attempt used the reference's
//! own literal default `state.climate.wIters=70`, producing `field[0..5]`
//! close to but NOT bit-identical to `golden_parity_carve.rs`'s fixture
//! (~1e-5 off) -- close enough to look like float noise, but this
//! project's own discipline (`cartalith-porting-discipline`) is explicit
//! that "close" isn't good enough for a harness cross-check. Root cause:
//! `golden_parity_carve.rs`'s own fixture was captured with
//! `p.climate.w_iters=12` (an explicit override for extraction speed, not
//! the real default), so wind/rain convergence -- and therefore the
//! carved `field` downstream -- genuinely differs at `wIters=70`. Setting
//! `state.climate.wIters=12` before extraction reproduced the fixture
//! exactly. Not a wrong-seed or wrong-code-path bug this time, just a
//! parameter mismatch -- recorded here so it isn't rediscovered.
//!
//! Case 2 is a NEW, larger fixture (gw=48 gh=40 seed=777 world=false),
//! added specifically because cases 0/1's tiny grids (154/192 cells)
//! genuinely produce zero nonzero corridor cells from the real reference
//! engine -- confirmed, not a bug (`buildRouteCorridors`'s own comment:
//! "SPARSE, like every other opportunity term" -- `CORRIDOR_KNEE=0.45` is
//! a strict threshold). An all-zero corridors fixture would technically
//! pass even with an inverted min/max in the flanking-barrier logic, so
//! case 2 exists to genuinely exercise the `gap > CORRIDOR_KNEE` branch
//! (203/1920 cells nonzero at this size).
//!
//! `build_raw_slope_field` (NOT `build_slope_field`, milestone 1's own
//! function) is used for the corridors input -- `currentSlopeField()`
//! (reference line 5661) returns raw, unscaled `slopeAt(x,y)`, distinct
//! from `currentSoil()`'s own inline `slopeAt(x,y)*GW` convention that
//! `build_slope_field` replicates. Confusing the two silently double- (or
//! under-) scales `buildRouteCorridors`'s cost field.
//!
//! Corridors/coast-SDF are continuous `f32` -- `1e-4` tolerance, matching
//! this crate's existing convention. Landmass-quality count is exact
//! (component count is an integer). `landmassQuality` itself is
//! continuous but built from that same exact partition, so also `1e-4`.

fn assert_close(actual: &[f32], expected: &[f32], label: &str) {
    const ATOL: f32 = 1e-4;
    const RTOL: f32 = 1e-4;
    assert_eq!(actual.len(), expected.len(), "{label}: length mismatch");
    for (i, (&a, &e)) in actual.iter().zip(expected.iter()).enumerate() {
        let tol = ATOL + RTOL * e.abs();
        assert!(
            (a - e).abs() <= tol,
            "{label} index {i}: got {a}, expected {e} (diff {}, tol {tol})",
            (a - e).abs()
        );
    }
}

